ection{Supporting material}\n     6\t\\label{app:machinery-all}\n     7\t\n     8\tThis appendix holds the material that supports Sections~\\ref{sec:equiv}\n     9\tand~\\ref{sec:search} without being needed to follow them.\n    10\t\n    11\t\\subsection{Edge cases and a gap in a published proof}\n    12\t\\label{app:edge}\n    13\t\n    14\tThree of the published equalities fail on degenerate inputs, and each failure\n    15\tis proved in Lean. On a nonempty graph with no edges, $\\ns=0$ while\n    16\t$\\vs+1=\\theta=1$, so both equalities of \\citet{LY2002ref9,LY2002ref10} need an\n    17\tedge~\\leanref{edgeless-ns}.\n    18\tFor a matrix with no 1s, $Z=0$ while $\\pw+1=1$. Lengauer's Theorem~4 fails at\n    19\t$K=0$ on an edgeless graph~\\leanref{vsg0}.\n    20\t\\citet[Thm~3.9]{LY2002ref6}, $t=\\ns$, fails literally on the $1\\times1$ matrix\n    21\t$[1]$~\\leanref{tns}.\n    22\t\n    23\tThe published proof of $\\vs\\le\\ns-1$ \\citep[Thm~4.1]{LY2002ref10} has a gap. It\n    24\torders the vertices by when they first receive a searcher and claims that every\n    25\tvertex counted at the worst cut still holds a searcher, ``since the vertices in\n    26\t$D_{i_0}$ are joined to vertices not yet cleared'' (p.~217). A strategy may place\n    27\ta searcher and remove it again before clearing anything, which recontaminates\n    28\tnothing. On $K_{1,3}$, placing and removing a searcher on each leaf before\n    29\tguarding the centre is an optimal recontamination-free strategy for which the\n    30\tclaim fails~\\leanref{kp}. The theorem is true. The\n    31\trepair orders the vertices by clearing time, as our Lean proof does, or uses the\n    32\tpaper's own normal form, its Corollary~2.4.\n    33\t\n    34\t\\subsection{A thirteenth member}\n    35\t\\label{app:thirteenth}\n    36\t\n    37\tProgressive black-white pebbling, which is not in Table~1, belongs to the exact\n    38\tcore. \\citet[Thm~3]{LY2002ref14} gives\n    39\t$\\vs(G)\\le K\\iff\\mathrm{pbw}(G_d)\\le K+2$ on the dag $G_d$ built from $G$; his\n    40\tTheorem~2 gives $\\mathrm{pbw}(D)=\\vs(D_u)+1$ on a dag $D$, where $D_u$ is the\n    41\tMOSP graph of the matrix whose column $v$ is $v$ with its predecessors; and the\n    42\tminimum progressive pebbling number of \\citet[Thm~3.1]{LY2002ref10} equals\n    43\t$\\pw+1$. All three are proved in Lean, each with its hypothesis: Lengauer's\n    44\tTheorem~2 in its instance form fails at $K=1$ on a dag with no arcs, and\n    45\t$\\mathrm{mpb}=\\ns$ fails on edgeless graphs~\\leanref{pebbling}.\n    46\t\n    47\t\\subsection{Why the published proof of the definite move fails}\n    48\t\\label{app:whyfails}\n    49\t\n    50\tThe proof moves $q$ to the front of a\n    51\tsolution and claims that after each prefix ``we have at most open(q, S) extra\n    52\tstacks open, but at least close(q, S) extra stacks closed'' (p.~6). At a prefix\n    53\t$T\\supseteq S$ that does not yet contain $q$, moving $q$ forward changes the open\n    54\tstacks by $b(T\\cup X)-b(T) = |N[q]\\setminus O(T)|-|X\\setminus T|$. At $T=S$ this\n    55\tis $\\open-\\close\\le0$. But customers of $X$ that the solution has \\emph{already}\n    56\tclosed are in $T$ and no longer count as extra stacks closed, while the stacks\n    57\t$q$ opens may still be new. In the example, a solution may begin with 3, after\n    58\twhich 4 is free: at $T=\\{2,3,4\\}$, moving 0 forward costs one more stack. A\n    59\tsecond, independent slip: even where the conclusion holds, the literal sequence\n    60\t$S \\mathbin{+\\!\\!+} [q] \\mathbin{+\\!\\!+} R'$ need not be a solution, because a\n    61\tcustomer that $q$ frees stays open until its turn; the argument needs the free\n    62\tmoves.\n    63\t\n    64\t\\subsection{The search for smaller counterexamples}\n    65\t\\label{app:minimality}\n    66\t\n    67\tThe smallest graph on which the rule loses the last solution has 14 customers:\n    68\tthere is none on any graph to 9 vertices, by exhaustion, and none on the 88,802\n    69\tconnected graphs on 10 vertices with at most 14 edges. The counterexample was\n    70\tfound by a search over a family of gadgets built from the failed proof, and\n    71\tminimised by deletion.\n    72\t\n    73\t\\subsection{The filter and the runs are sound}\n    74\t\\label{app:machinery}\n    75\t\n    76\t\\begin{proposition}\\label{prop:filter}\n    77\tAt every state with the invariant, for every set $Q$ of refuted old moves, the\n    78\tfilter definite $\\to$ subset $\\to$ better with both repairs is node-sound~\\leanref{filter}.\n    79\t\\end{proposition}\n    80\t\\begin{proof}\n    81\tIf the definite move fires, Theorem~\\ref{thm:repaired} applies. Otherwise the\n    82\tsubset rule keeps a survivor with a solution; let $w$ be the earliest. If the\n    83\tbetter move dropped $w$, it cited an earlier survivor that the repaired\n    84\targument proves solvable, contradicting the choice of $w$.\n    85\t\\end{proof}\n    86\t\n    87\t\\begin{theorem}[runs are sound]\\label{thm:runs}\n    88\tLet the filter be node-sound and return only playable customers. If at the start\n    89\tof a run the invariant holds, every memo entry is a genuine refutation, and\n    90\tevery old move is refuted, then the run leaves only genuine memo entries, and if\n    91\tit answers \\emph{false} at $S$ then $\\neg\\Sol_k(S)$. This holds for any child\n    92\torder and any memo policy, with the old move on or off~\\leanref{runs}.\n    93\t\\end{theorem}\n    94\tThe proof is an induction over the run, in the order in which subtrees complete:\n    95\tevery old move a node holds is a sibling subtree that completed with \\emph{false}\n    96\tearlier in the same run, or an inherited one that passed the reinsertion test.\n     1\t% Appendix A: the formal names (loop0009 item 01). Every Lean-name footnote of\n     2\t% the draft moved here; the text cites an entry as (Appendix A, A.n) with\n     3\t% the leanref macro. check_lean.py and