n Lean. On a nonempty graph with no edges, $\\ns=0$ while\n    16\t$\\vs+1=\\theta=1$, so both equalities of \\citet{LY2002ref9,LY2002ref10} need an\n    17\tedge~\\leanref{edgeless-ns}.\n    18\tFor a matrix with no 1s, $Z=0$ while $\\pw+1=1$. Lengauer's Theorem~4 fails at\n    19\t$K=0$ on an edgeless graph~\\leanref{vsg0}.\n    20\t\\citet[Thm~3.9]{LY2002ref6}, $t=\\ns$, fails literally on the $1\\times1$ matrix\n    21\t$[1]$~\\leanref{tns}.\n    22\t\n    23\tThe published proof of $\\vs\\le\\ns-1$ \\citep[Thm~4.1]{LY2002ref10} has a gap. It\n    24\torders the vertices by when they first receive a searcher and claims that every\n    25\tvertex counted at the worst cut still holds a searcher, ``since the vertices in\n    26\t$D_{i_0}$ are joined to vertices not yet cleared'' (p.~217). A strategy may place\n    27\ta searcher and remove it again before clearing anything, which recontaminates\n    28\tnothing. On $K_{1,3}$, placing and removing a searcher on each leaf before\n    29\tguarding the centre is an optimal recontamination-free strategy for which the\n    30\tclaim fails~\\leanref{kp}. The theorem is true. The\n    31\trepair orders the vertices by clearing time, as our Lean proof does, or uses the\n    32\tpaper's own normal form, its Corollary~2.4.\n    33\t\n    34\t\\subsection{A thirteenth member}\n    35\t\\label{app:thirteenth}\n    36\t\n    37\tProgressive black-white pebbling, which is not in Table~1, belongs to the exact\n    38\tcore. \\citet[Thm~3]{LY2002ref14} gives\n    39\t$\\vs(G)\\le K\\iff\\mathrm{pbw}(G_d)\\le K+2$ on the dag $G_d$ built from $G$; his\n    40\tTheorem~2 gives $\\mathrm{pbw}(D)=\\vs(D_u)+1$ on a dag $D$, where $D_u$ is the\n    41\tMOSP graph of the matrix whose column $v$ is $v$ with its predecessors; and the\n    42\tminimum progressive pebbling number of \\citet[Thm~3.1]{LY2002ref10} equals\n    43\t$\\pw+1$. All three are proved in Lean, each with its hypothesis: Lengauer's\n    44\tTheorem~2 in its instance form fails at $K=1$ on a dag with no arcs, and\n    45\t$\\mathrm{mpb}=\\ns$ fails on edgeless graphs~\\leanref{pebbling}.\n    46\t\n    47\t\\subsection{Why the published proof of the definite move fails}\n    48\t\\label{app:whyfails}\n    49\t\n    50\tThe proof moves $q$ to the front of a\n    51\tsolution and claims that after each prefix ``we have at most open(q, S) extra\n    52\tstacks open, but at least close(q, S) extra stacks closed'' (p.~6). At a prefix\n    53\t$T\\supseteq S$ that does not yet contain $q$, moving $q$ forward changes the open\n    54\tstacks by $b(T\\cup X)-b(T) = |N[q]\\setminus O(T)|-|X\\setminus T|$. At $T=S$ this\n    55\tis $\\open-\\close\\le0$. But customers of $X$ that the solution has \\emph{already}\n    56\tclosed are in $T$ and no longer count as extra stacks closed, while the stacks\n    57\t$q$ opens may still be new. In the example, a solution may begin with 3, after\n    58\twhich 4 is free: at $T=\\{2,3,4\\}$, moving 0 forward costs one more stack. A\n    59\tsecond, independent slip: even where the conclusion holds, the literal sequence\n    60\t$S \\mathbin{+\\!\\!+} [q] \\mathbin{+\\!\\!+} R'$ need not be a solution, because a\n    61\tcustomer that $q$ frees stays open until its turn; the argument needs the free\n    62\tmoves.\n    63\t\n    64\t\\subsection{The search for smaller counterexamples}\n    65\t\\label{app:minimality}\n    66\t\n    67\tThe smallest graph on which the rule loses the last solution has 14 customers:\n    68\tthere is none on any graph to 9 vertices, by exhaustion, and none on the 88,802\n    69\tconnected graphs on 10 vertices with at most 14 edges. The counterexample was\n    70\tfound by a search over a family of gadgets built from the failed proof, and\n    71\tminimised by deletion.\n    72\t\n    73\t\\subsection{The filter and the runs are sound}\n    74\t\\label{app:machinery}\n    75\t\n    76\t\\begin{proposition}\\label{prop:filter}\n    77\tAt every state with the invariant, for every set $Q$ of refuted old moves, the\n    78\tfilter definite $\\to$ subset $\\to$ better with both repairs is node-sound~\\leanref{filter}.\n    79\t\\end{proposition}\n    80\t\\begin{proof}\n    81\tIf the definite move fires, Theorem~\\ref{thm:repaired} applies. Otherwise the\n    82\tsubset rule keeps a survivor with a solution; let $w$ be the earliest. If the\n    83\tbetter move dropped $w$, it cited an earlier survivor that the repaired\n    84\targument proves solvable, contradicting the choice of $w$.\n    85\t\\end{proof}\n    86\t\n    87\t\\begin{theorem}[runs are sound]\\label{thm:runs}\n    88\tLet the filter be node-sound and return only playable customers. If at the start\n    89\tof a run the invariant holds, every memo entry is a genuine refutation, and\n    90\tevery old move is refuted, then the run leaves only genuine memo entries, and if\n    91\tit answers \\emph{false} at $S$ then $\\neg\\Sol_k(S)$. This holds for any child\n    92\torder and any memo policy, with the old move on or off~\\leanref{runs}.\n    93\t\\end{theorem}\n    94\tThe proof is an induction over the run, in the order in which subtrees complete:\n    95\tevery old move a node holds is a sibling subtree that completed with \\emph{false}\n    96\tearlier in the same run, or an inherited one that passed the reinsertion test.\n     1\t% Appendix A: the formal names (loop0009 item 01). Every Lean-name footnote of\n     2\t% the draft moved here; the text cites an entry as (Appendix A, A.n) with\n     3\t% the leanref macro. check_lean.py and check_axioms.py read the leannames\n     4\t% cells. Axiom line printed by paper2/axiom_check.lean (`make axioms`).\n     5\t\\section{Formal names}\n     6\t\\label{app:lean}\n     7\t\n     8\tEvery theorem, lemma and counterexample of this paper is checked in the Lean~4\n     9\tproof assistant with the Mathlib library, unless the text says otherwise. The\n    10\treader need not read Lean to follow the paper. This appendix is for a reader\n    11\twho wants to find the formal statement of a result: Table~\\ref{tab:lean} maps\n    12\teach result, in the order in which it appears, to the names of the Lean\n    13\tdeclarations that state it. Where a row cites a definition rather than a\n    14\ttheorem, it is the definition the text uses.\n    15\t\n    16\tAll the theorems named depend only on the three standard axioms of Lean's\n    17\tclassical logic, \\texttt{propext}, \\texttt{Classical.choice} and\n    18\t\\texttt{Quot.sound}; an axiom check in the repository prints this for every\n    19\ttheorem named in the paper. The development has one \\texttt{sorry}, a\n    20\tconjecture about lower bounds that no result of this paper uses. The part of\n    21\tthe development behind Section~\\ref{sec:search} has no \\texttt{sorry} at all.\n    22\t\n    23\t{\\small\n    24\t\\begin{longtable}{@{}>{\\raggedright}p{1.0cm}>{\\raggedright}p{6.6cm}>{\\raggedright\\arraybackslash}p{8.0cm}@{}}\n    25\t\\caption{Each result of the paper and its formal statement in Lean.}\\label{tab:lean}\\\\\n    26\t\\toprule\n    27\tEntry & Result in the paper & Lean names \\\\\n    28\t\\midrule\n    29\t\\endfirsthead\n    30\t\\toprule\n    31\tEntry & Result in the paper & Lean names \\\\\n    32\t\\midrule\n    33\t\\endhead\n    34\t\\bottomrule\n    35\t\\endfoot\n    36\t\\leangroup{Section~\\ref{sec:equiv}, the equivalences}\n    37\t\\leanentry{clique}{Lemma~\\ref{lem:clique}, cliques sit in a bag} \\leannames{PathDecomposition.exists_bag_of_isClique} \\\\\n    38\t\\leanentry{vs}{Theorem~\\ref{thm:vs}, vertex separation is pathwidth} \\leannames{vertexSeparation_eq_pathwidth} \\\\\n    39\t\\leanentry{mosp}{Theorem~\\ref{thm:mosp}, MOSP is pathwidth plus one} \\leannames{MOSPInstance.mospValue_eq_pathwidth_add_one} \\\\\n    40\t\\leanentry{star}{The pattern graph gives no bound: the star $K_{1,4}$ (after Theorem~\\ref{thm:mosp})} \\leannames{star_mospValue, star_pathwidth_agreementGraph, star_refutes_pattern_graph_bound} \\\\\n    41\t\\leanentry{core-gml}{Theorem~\\ref{thm:core}(1), gate matrix layout} \\leannames{NetGateMatrix.tracks_eq_mospValue, NetGateMatrix.tracks_eq_pathwidth_add_one} \\\\\n    42\t\\leanentry{core-it}{Theorem~\\ref{thm:core}(2), interval thickness} \\leannames{intervalThickness_eq_pathwidth_add_one} \\\\\n    43\t\\leanentry{core-logic}{Theorem~\\ref{thm:core}(3), one-dimensional logic} \\leannames{LogicArray.tracks_eq_intervalThickness, LogicArray.tracks_eq_pathwidth_add_one} \\\\\n    44\t\\leanentry{core-narrow}{Theorem~\\ref{thm:core}(4), narrowness} \\leannames{narrowness_eq_pathwidth_add_one} \\\\\n    45\t\\leanentry{core-ns}{Theorem~\\ref{thm:core}(5), node search} \\leannames{nodeSearchMonotonicity, nodeSearch_eq_vertexSeparation_add_one, nodeSearch_chain} \\\\\n    46\t\\leanentry{core-fold}{Theorem~\\ref{thm:core}(6), multiple folding} \\leannames{NetGateMatrix.foldTracks_eq_pathwidth_add_one} \\\\\n    47\t\\leanentry{core-vsg}{Theorem~\\ref{thm:core}(7), Lengauer's vertex separator game} \\leannames{vsg_eq_max} \\\\\n    48\t\\leanentry{bands}{Theorem~\\ref{thm:bands}, the bands} \\leannames{pathwidth_le_splitBandwidth_le_pathwidth_add_one, vertexSeparation_le_edgeSearch_le_add_two, vertexSeparation_le_progressiveEdgeSearch_le_add_two} \\\\\n    49\t\\leanentry{false-pla}{Theorem~\\ref{thm:false}(1), PLA folding} \\leannames{plaTracks_idMatrix_five, plaTracks_idMatrix_unbounded, plaTracks_pathMatrix_unbounded} \\\\\n    50\t\\leanentry{false-cw}{Theorem~\\ref{thm:false}(2), edge separation read as cutwidth} \\leannames{cutwidth_unbounded, modCutwidth_unbounded} \\\\\n    51\t\\leangroup{Section~\\ref{sec:search}, the search}\n    52\t\\leanentry{defs}{Step cost, $P_k$ and $\\Sol_k$ (Section~\\ref{sec:search-def})} \\leannames{stepCost, Solvable, SearchSol} \\\\\n    53\t\\leanentry{F}{Lemma~\\ref{lem:F}, free moves never hurt} \\leannames{solvable_iff_searchSol_cl} \\\\\n    54\t\\leanentry{decides}{Theorem~\\ref{thm:decides}, the search decides pathwidth} \\leannames{searchSol_empty_iff_pathwidth_add_one_le, searchSol_mospGraph_iff_mospValue_le} \\\\\n    55\t\\leanentry{nodesound}{Node-soundness of a filter (Section~\\ref{sec:search-def})} \\leannames{NodeSoundAt} \\\\\n    56\t\\leanentry{premise}{Lemma~\\ref{lem:premise}, what the premise says} \\leannames{closeCount_eq, isDefinite_iff} \\\\\n    57\t\\leanentry{cex}{Counterexample~\\ref{cex:definite}} \\leannames{definiteMove_counterexample, cexFamily_closed} \\\\\n    58\t\\leanentry{thm1false}{Chu and Stuckey's Theorem~1, under both readings, and Chu's Theorem~6.3.6 are false} \\leannames{chuStuckey_theorem1_false, chuStuckey_theorem1_false_literal, chuThesis_theorem636_false} \\\\\n    59\t\\leanentry{fink}{Fink's restatement of the definite move is false} \\leannames{fink_theorem1_false} \\\\\n    60\t\\leanentry{hered}{The hereditarily definite premise, and its failure in Counterexample~\\ref{cex:definite}} \\leannames{IsHereditarilyDefinite, not_isHereditarilyDefinite_cex} \\\\","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
