{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01YLJHJ3zYJgMj8SmrRqeE9g","type":"tool_result","content":"    60\tlargest bag minus one. The least width over all path decompositions is the\n    61\t\\emph{pathwidth} of the graph, here 2. The construction works in both\n    62\tdirections, so the fewest open stacks is always the pathwidth plus one\n    63\t(Theorem~\\ref{thm:mosp}).\n    64\t\n    65\t\\begin{table}[tbp]\n    66\t\\centering\n    67\t\\caption{The Linhares--Yanasse table, reproduced from \\citet[Table~1,\n    68\tp.~1764]{LY2002} with its row order, wording and references as printed, and\n    69\tits caption: ``Display of a family of equivalent problems studied\n    70\tindependently on the literature''. The shaded column is ours. It gives the\n    71\trelation to pathwidth ($\\pw$) that is true, proved in Section~\\ref{sec:equiv}\n    72\t(Table~\\ref{tab:master}). Eight rows are exact, counting the pathwidth row\n    73\titself; two are bands; two are false as the table states them.}\n    74\t\\label{tab:ly2002}\n    75\t\\small\n    76\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}lll>{\\columncolor{black!7}}l@{}}\n    77\t\\toprule\n    78\tProblem & Discipline & References & This paper \\\\\n    79\t\\midrule\n    80\tMOSP & Operations research & \\citet{Yanasse1997b}; \\citet{LY2002ref4} & exact: $\\pw+1$ \\\\\n    81\tGate matrix layout & VLSI design & \\citet{LY2002ref6}; \\citet{LY2002ref8} & exact: $\\pw+1$ \\\\\n    82\tOne-dimensional logic & VLSI design & \\citet{LY2002ref7} & exact: $\\pw+1$ \\\\\n    83\tPLA folding & VLSI design & \\citet{LY2002ref6} & false$^{a}$ \\\\\n    84\tInterval thickness & Graph theory & \\citet{LY2002ref5} & exact: $\\pw+1$ \\\\\n    85\tNode search game & Graph theory & \\citet{LY2002ref9} & exact: $\\pw+1$ \\\\\n    86\tEdge search game & Graph theory & \\citet{LY2002ref10} & band: $\\pw$ to $\\pw+2$ \\\\\n    87\tNarrowness & Graph theory & \\citet{LY2002ref11} & exact: $\\pw+1$ \\\\\n    88\tSplit bandwidth & Graph theory & \\citet{LY2002ref12} & band: $\\pw$ to $\\pw+1$ \\\\\n    89\tGraph path-width & Graph theory & \\citet{LY2002ref13} & exact: $\\pw$ (reference) \\\\\n    90\tEdge separation & Graph theory & \\citet{LY2002ref14} & false$^{b}$ \\\\\n    91\tVertex separation & Graph theory & \\citet{LY2002ref13} & exact: $\\pw$ \\\\\n    92\t\\bottomrule\n    93\t\\multicolumn{4}{@{}p{\\linewidth}@{}}{\\footnotesize $^{a}$Exact ($\\pw+1$) for\n    94\tmultiple folding, with any number of nets per track.\n    95\t$^{b}$As cutwidth; the vertex separator game of the same reference is exact\n    96\t($\\pw$, for graphs with an edge).}\n    97\t\\end{tabular}\n    98\t\\end{table}\n    99\t\n   100\t\\citet{LY2002} collected twelve such problems, from operations research, VLSI\n   101\tdesign and graph theory, in the table that Table~\\ref{tab:ly2002} reproduces.\n   102\tEach computes, on corresponding inputs, ``a function $f(\\Pi)$ that is either\n   103\tequal to the number of open stacks or closely related to it (plus or minus\n   104\tone)'' \\citep[p.~1764]{LY2002}. The table gives a reference for each problem,\n   105\tbut no proofs and no graph of which problem reduces to which. ``Plus or minus\n   106\tone'' has two readings. Under the \\emph{literal} reading, $f$ is within one of\n   107\tthe number of open stacks on every input. Under the \\emph{fixed-offset}\n   108\treading, $f$ equals the number of open stacks plus one constant, the same on\n   109\tevery input. Only the second makes solving one problem the same as solving\n   110\tanother. We call the twelve \\emph{the pathwidth complex}, because pathwidth is\n   111\tboth the name the literature uses most (Section~\\ref{sec:names}) and the\n   112\tquantity through which every true relation in the table is proved\n   113\t(Section~\\ref{sec:equiv}). This paper asks: \\emph{which of the equalities in\n   114\tthe Linhares--Yanasse table are true, and what follows when results,\n   115\talgorithms and benchmarks are moved between the twelve problems through\n   116\tpathwidth?}\n   117\t\n   118\tThe table has a history that explains why the question is still open. It was\n   119\tfirst printed as Table~2.1 of the first author's doctoral thesis\n   120\t\\citep[p.~13]{Linhares2001}, supervised by the second author, which proved MOSP\n   121\tNP-hard and equal to gate matrix layout. Its rows were collected over the years\n   122\tof the doctorate, while reading the literature of each field. Each came with a\n   123\treference and nothing else. No row's relation to open stacks was checked, no\n   124\tpublished proof was restated, and no graph said which problem reduces to which.\n   125\tThe table appeared again, row for row, as Table~1 of \\citet{LY2002}, and it has\n   126\tbeen read since as a statement of equivalence.\n   127\t\\citet{GarciaDeLaBandaStuckey2007}, for instance, credit the speed of their\n   128\tsearch in this journal to ``the fact that the problem is fixed parameter\n   129\ttractable (Linhares and Yanasse 2002)''. So the table is already used to\n   130\tjustify algorithms, and whether it is true matters. If it is true, every result\n   131\tabout any one of the twelve problems transfers to all the others. Looking back,\n   132\tthe table was the thesis's most important finding, and this article does what\n   133\tthe thesis did not: it states each problem precisely, proves what is true and\n   134\trefutes what is not. The communities that could have checked the table\n   135\trarely read each other: of 844 works that cite the table's papers,\n   136\tonly six cite both an open-stacks paper and a graph-theory paper\n   137\t(Section~\\ref{sec:names}).\n   138\t\n   139\t\\paragraph{What we find.}\n   140\t\\begin{itemize}\n   141\t\\item \\emph{The table, settled.} Eight of the twelve rows are exact, the\n   142\t  pathwidth row itself counted as one of them: the other seven equal pathwidth\n   143\t  or pathwidth plus one on every input, outside degenerate cases such as an\n   144\t  empty graph. Two rows, split bandwidth and edge\n   145\t  search, lie in bands one and two wide. Two rows, PLA folding and edge\n   146\t  separation, are false as stated, with counterexamples. A variant of each\n   147\t  false row is exact (Section~\\ref{sec:equiv}).\n   148\t\\item \\emph{One name.} The literature has settled on ``pathwidth'', which has\n   149\t  more than three times as many relevant works as the other eleven names\n   150\t  together. Besides pathwidth, the most active community over 2005--2024 is the\n   151\t  open-stacks one, and the exact search of Section~\\ref{sec:search} came\n   152\t  from it\n   153\t  (Section~\\ref{sec:names}).\n   154\t\\item \\emph{A repaired search.} The best exact algorithm for MOSP, the search of\n   155\t  \\citet{ChuStuckey2009}, is also a pathwidth algorithm. Two of its published\n   156\t  dominance theorems are false, as are their later restatements\n   157\t  \\citep{Chu2011,Fink2012}. The correct rule was already known in the exact\n   158\t  pathwidth literature, and we show that it can be tested by one bipartite\n   159\t  matching per candidate. The repaired search is proved sound. Over each\n   160\t  benchmark set it changes the total number of search nodes by at most 2\\%, and no wrong answer of the\n   161\t  published rules on a whole instance is known (Section~\\ref{sec:search}).\n   162\t\\item \\emph{Benchmarks re-certified, and a dataset.} The repaired search\n   163\t  re-proved all 115 benchmark optima that rested on the published rules, and\n   164\t  none changed (as of 6 October 2026). We release 17,714 graphs, distinct up to\n   165\t  isomorphism, from twenty benchmark collections of the twelve problems; 16,087\n   166\t  have a certified pathwidth with a layout that attains it, and so a certified\n   167\t  value for every exact problem in the table (Section~\\ref{sec:results}).\n   168\t\\end{itemize}\n   169\t\n   170\t\\paragraph{How to read the paper.} Section~\\ref{sec:equiv} states the twelve\n   171\tproblems and proves what is true. Section~\\ref{sec:names} measures which names\n   172\tand communities the literature uses. Section~\\ref{sec:search} reads the exact\n   173\topen-stacks search as a pathwidth algorithm, finds the two false rules and\n   174\tproves the repaired search sound. Section~\\ref{sec:results} reports its cost\n   175\tand the dataset, and Section~\\ref{sec:closing} answers the question above.\n   176\tEvery theorem we state is machine-checked in the Lean~4 proof assistant. The\n   177\treader need not read Lean: the proofs are given in the text, and\n   178\tAppendix~\\ref{app:lean} maps each result to its formal statement.\n   179\tAppendix~\\ref{app:machinery-all} holds the supporting material, and the data,\n   180\tcode and proofs are described at the end of the paper (page~\\pageref{sec:data}).\npaper2/better_hunt_gen.py\npaper2/certificate_check.py\npaper2/certificates.py\npaper2/citation_graph.py\npaper2/complex_check.py\npaper2/counterexample_figure.py\npaper2/dataset.py\npaper2/dataset_check.py\npaper2/dataset_price.py\npaper2/definite_hunt_gen.py\npaper2/fink_check.py\npaper2/intro_figure.py\npaper2/prior_art_sweep.py\npaper2/relevance.py\npaper2/search_check.py\npaper2/section2_figures.py\npaper2/section3_figure.py\npaper2/solver_fix_bench.py\npaper2/solver_fix_c_check.py\npaper2/solver_fix_check.py\npaper2/solver_fix_cost.py\npaper2/solver_fix_cost_tables.py\npaper2/solver_fix_provenance.py\npaper2/solver_fix_pw_check.py\npaper2/solver_fix_recheck.py\npaper2/solver_fix_soundness.py\npaper2/solver_fix_split.py\npaper2/thesis_check.py\npaper2/trends.py\npaper2/section3_figure.py:3:    python -m paper2.section3_figure          # writes figures/sec3_fig1_chain.{pdf,png}\npaper2/section3_figure.py:136:    out = FIGS / \"sec3_fig1_chain.pdf\"\npaper2/section3_figure.py:138:    fig.savefig(FIGS / \"sec3_fig1_chain.png\", dpi=200, bbox_inches=\"tight\", pad_inches=0